<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin-bottom: 10px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Time for Exercise – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Time for Exercise</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Like Walking in the Afternoon.</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Compose messages to invite peers to a performance, event, or activity. Interactive: Exchange messages, letters, or texts with classmates or peers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can compose an invitation to a sports event (e.g., "Come to our sports day!"). Can exchange details in a message (e.g., "The sports day is on Friday at 10 a.m."). Can exchange simple messages about activities and events (e.g., "Let's take a walk!").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and displays flashcards or images of various sports and activities (e.g., people walking, cycling, playing football, swimming). The teacher asks, "What do you see?" and "Do you like this activity?" Students respond with simple phrases like "I see people cycling" or "Yes, I like swimming." This activates prior vocabulary related to "Time for Exercise" and "I Like Walking in the Afternoon." The teacher writes key words on the board (e.g., <em>walk, cycle, swim, football, activity</em>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher shows a simple written invitation on the board: "Hi [Friend's Name], Let's go cycling in the afternoon! Can you come? It's fun! See you! [Your Name]." The teacher reads it aloud, pointing to each word, modeling clear pronunciation, especially /k/ in "can" and /s/ in "swim." The teacher explains that this is a message to invite a friend to an activity. The teacher asks, "What is this for?" (To invite a friend). "What activity?" (Cycling).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is this a letter or a short message?" (A short message.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does it ask a question?" (Yes, "Can you come?")</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher reinforces that today's focus is on writing these types of messages.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Time for Exercise".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher displays several short, simple written invitations or text messages projected on the board or on handouts. Examples include:
                    <br>1. "Hi Ana! Do you like swimming? Let's go to the gym on Friday. It's exciting! Can you come? Luis."
                    <br>2. "Hey Mark! I like running in the afternoon. Let's run together! You can do it! See you at 4 p.m. Sarah."
                    <br>3. "Hello friend! What type of exercise do you like? I like hiking. Come hiking on Saturday morning. It's healthy and fun! Ben."
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will understand written messages inviting peers to activities, identifying key details and expressions of preference. This prepares them to compose similar messages.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the invitations individually or in pairs. The teacher then asks gist and detail questions:
                </p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Who is inviting Ana?" (Luis.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What activity does Mark like?" (Running.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When is Ben going hiking?" (Saturday morning.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which message talks about the gym?" (The first one.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What phrase means 'Do you want to come'?" ("Can you come?")</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher highlights grammatical structures like "Do you like...?", "I like...", "Let's...", "Can you come?" and vocabulary such as <em>gym, running, hiking, swimming, healthy, fun, exciting, afternoon, friend, every day</em>.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice writing sentences using modal verbs (can, cannot), present simple questions/affirmative sentences, and WH-questions with verb + -ing, along with target vocabulary (e.g., <em>walk, exercise, afternoon, gym, sport, activity, friend, football, swimming, running, healthy, active, fun, exciting</em>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Fill-in-the-Blanks Invitations:</b> The teacher provides a handout with incomplete invitation messages. Students fill in the blanks using words from a provided word bank or their own ideas based on the context.
                        <br>Example: "Hi ___, Do you like ___? Let's go ___ in the ___. Can you ___? It's ___! See you at ___ p.m. ___." (Word bank: <em>friend, swimming, running, afternoon, come, fun, healthy, exciting</em>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Sentence Scramble:</b> The teacher writes jumbled sentences on the board or provides them on a worksheet. Students reorder the words to form grammatically correct invitation sentences.
                        <br>Example: "afternoon / like / I / in / walking / the / ." → "I like walking in the afternoon."
                        <br>"come / can / you / ?" → "Can you come?"
                        <br>"exercise / type / what / like / you / do / of / ?" → "What type of exercise do you like?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Guided Invitation Writing:</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a scenario (e.g., "Invite a friend to play football," "Invite a friend to go dancing"). They use sentence frames and a list of target vocabulary (<em>play, dance, jump, throw, catch, kick, hit, active, fit, sport, activity, friend</em>) to draft a simple invitation message. The teacher circulates, offering support with spelling and grammar, ensuring the use of "Let's...", "Do you like...?", "I like...", and "Can you come?".</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Time for Exercise".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual Written Product – "My Invitation Message."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Students imagine they want to invite a classmate to a sports event or an exercise activity. They will individually compose a short invitation message. The teacher explains the task: "Write a message to invite your friend to an activity you like."</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students choose an activity (e.g., <em>walking, cycling, swimming, playing football, running, dancing, hiking, horseback riding</em>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They must include:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A greeting (e.g., "Hi [Friend's Name]!").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">An expression of preference (e.g., "I like walking in the afternoon.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">An invitation (e.g., "Let's take a walk!").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A question about availability (e.g., "Can you come?").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Details (e.g., "The walk is on Friday at 4 p.m.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">An encouraging phrase (e.g., "It's healthy and fun! You can do it!").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A closing (e.g., "See you! [Your Name]").</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students write their messages on a piece of paper. The teacher encourages them to use the vocabulary and grammar practiced, including segmenting multisyllabic words like "exciting" if they use them.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and peer assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students exchange their written invitation messages with a partner. Each student reads their partner's invitation and provides simple feedback using a checklist. Then, they attempt to write a short reply message (e.g., "Yes, I can come! See you then.") or "No, I cannot come. I like swimming.")</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist/Peer Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Includes a greeting and closing? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses an invitation phrase ("Let's...")? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Asks "Can you come?" or "Do you like...?" (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Includes specific details (day/time)? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses at least 3 target vocabulary words (e.g., <em>walk, afternoon, healthy, fun, gym, sport</em>)? (Yes/No)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following prompts on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new word I used in my invitation today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to write an invitation? Why?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of writing an invitation is the most important?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What activity would I like to invite a family member to do?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The modeling and guided practice worked well, as students were able to construct invitations with the target structures. Many students successfully included the activity, time, and question. Some struggled with sentence structure and remembering to include encouraging phrases like 'You can do it!' or adjectives like 'exciting'. I need to provide more explicit sentence starters for the next writing task, or pre-teach common encouraging phrases."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The pronunciation focus on /k/ and /s/ in modeling was noted, but students focused on the written form. I will integrate a quick review of these sounds before the next speaking-focused activity."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Peer assessment was effective for identifying minor errors in detail (e.g., missing time) but not always for grammar. I will provide clearer error codes or specific aspects for peers to check for in the future."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will summarize the types of activities they invited friends to and discuss the benefits of exercise based on their written messages. They will also practice explaining their written invitations to other classmates, combining their writing and speaking skills for the 21st Century Skills Project.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>